Greyhollow clings to the shoulder of a grey mountain, a town of stone houses and mine adits, where the ring of smiths' hammers is the town's own heartbeat. The folk here worship the Anvil Father, and His temple stands over the deepest workings, its forge fires never cold. Brakka Ironvein came to Greyhollow a dwarf of the hills, and for six years he has stood guard at that temple with shield and longsword, a watcher at the gate of the town's faith.

Three days ago three miners went down into the old north workings — workings sealed a generation ago, after whatever happened there — and none came back. Last night a fourth man staggered up out of the dark alone, babbling about lights that walk, and died before the morning. The town council has voted to brick up the mouth of the mine at sunrise, missing men or no. So Prior Hessa, an old dwarf who has seen too many men go down and not enough come up, has asked Brakka to go down before then and bring back whoever can still be brought back.

Now it is late afternoon, and Brakka stands at the mine mouth with a lantern borrowed from the temple in one hand. Behind him the last light slants across the snow. Before him the tunnel gapes dark, the old timbers of its frame grey and dry as bone. A faded wooden sign is nailed to one post. To one side the council's masons have already stacked brick and mortar, waiting for the morning.

What do you do?
